@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Components
@using SpaceSails.Core

@* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   #949 · WHAT THE PLOTTING PANEL IS FOR, SAID IN SIX PARAGRAPHS, WITHOUT LEAVING THE GAME.

   Owner, 2026-08-18, posting a screenshot of his own multi-step plan: "We should have a help page where we
   show multi-step plan and the use of the schrub and burn. New player seeing this image would understand
   how to play. I really like the increment - and decrement options."

   THIS IS NOT THE EIGHT-STEP PAGE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE REASON IT EXISTS. /help/nav (#972) is
   the walkthrough: eight steps, a drawn sketch each, read once before you start. It opens in a SECOND TAB,
   because a full-page route over /map would take the game off the screen — which is exactly the wrong
   thing to do to somebody who is stuck mid-plan with the panel open and a question about the row in front
   of him. This is that question answered where it is asked: a card over the map, thirty seconds, gone on
   Escape, with the panel still underneath it. The card hands the reader on to the page for the long
   version; the page is still the reference.

   TWO RULES IT IS BUILT UNDER, and the second one is the one with a guard behind it.

   1. NO PICTURE OF THE PANEL. Not a screenshot, and not a sketch either — the sketches belong to the page,
      where there is a column's width to draw them in. Here the illustration IS the label: every face a
      captain has to hunt for on the real panel is printed as a chip, at the size it is printed on the
      button, so what he reads here is literally what he is looking for.

   2. EVERY FACE IS BORROWED, NEVER TYPED. Each chip below is a NodeFrame / ArrivalStepRule call — the
      SAME call the panel makes to draw that button. Rename a button and this card renames with it; type a
      face here by hand and TheHelpCardTeachesTodaysPanelTests goes red. A help page is the easiest place
      in this codebase for the repo's own named bug class to hide (a sentence reporting one thing while
      the sim does another), because nothing else in the client is made of sentences ABOUT the client.
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ *@

<div class="plotting-help">

    <p class="ph-lede">
        You never steer to Mars. You throw the ship at where Mars is going to be, then check your work —
        here, with the sky frozen and nothing at stake yet.
    </p>

    @* ── 1 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
    <section class="ph-step">
        <span class="ph-num" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
        <h3 class="ph-head">Say where you are going</h3>
        <p>
            Click a planet, moon or haven on the map, or give the order at the Captain's desk
            (<kbd>0</kbd>). Everything the panel tells you from here is measured against that one choice.
        </p>
    </section>

    @* ── 2 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
    <section class="ph-step">
        <span class="ph-num" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
        <h3 class="ph-head">The plan is a list of steps</h3>
        <p>
            <span class="ph-chip ph-chip-key">🗺 Plot</span> pauses the sim and opens the table. Cast off,
            burn, sling, skim, arrive — each is a row, and each row opens into a sub-panel that owns its own
            buttons. Nothing on this table spends a gram or a minute until you leave it.
        </p>
    </section>

    @* ── 3 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
    <section class="ph-step">
        <span class="ph-num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
        <h3 class="ph-head">The scrub is your finger on the timeline</h3>
        <p>
            Drag <span class="ph-chip">@NodeFrame.ScrubLabel</span> and the whole system runs forward as
            ghosts. Every button that says <em>at scrub</em> acts at the moment you are pointing at:
        </p>
        <p class="ph-chips">
            <span class="ph-chip ph-chip-key">@NodeFrame.AddBurnAtScrubButton</span>
            <span class="ph-chip">@ArrivalStepRule.AddAtScrubButton(ArrivalStepRule.ArrivalKind.Orbit)</span>
            <span class="ph-chip">@ArrivalStepRule.AddAtScrubButton(ArrivalStepRule.ArrivalKind.Dock)</span>
        </p>
        <p>
            Move the finger, press the button, and the step lands where you were pointing.
        </p>
    </section>

    @* ── 4 · THE ONE THE OWNER ASKED FOR BY NAME ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
    <section class="ph-step">
        <span class="ph-num" aria-hidden="true">4</span>
        <h3 class="ph-head">Tune a burn without typing</h3>
        <p>
            Every burn row carries three pairs. This is the part you will spend the most time on, and it
            needs no keyboard at all.
        </p>
        <dl class="ph-pairs">
            <dt>aim</dt>
            <dd>
                <span class="ph-chip">@NodeFrame.NudgeLabel(-1)</span>
                <span class="ph-chip">@NodeFrame.NudgeLabel(1)</span>
                — swing it off wherever it points now.
            </dd>
            <dt>size</dt>
            <dd>
                <span class="ph-chip">@NodeFrame.NudgeMagnitudeLabel(-1, true)</span>
                <span class="ph-chip">@NodeFrame.NudgeMagnitudeLabel(-1, false)</span>
                <span class="ph-chip">@NodeFrame.NudgeMagnitudeLabel(1, false)</span>
                <span class="ph-chip">@NodeFrame.NudgeMagnitudeLabel(1, true)</span>
                — spend more or fewer pulses.
            </dd>
            <dt>when</dt>
            <dd>
                <span class="ph-chip">@NodeFrame.NudgeEpochLabel(-1, true)</span>
                <span class="ph-chip">@NodeFrame.NudgeEpochLabel(-1, false)</span>
                <span class="ph-chip">@NodeFrame.NudgeEpochLabel(1, false)</span>
                <span class="ph-chip">@NodeFrame.NudgeEpochLabel(1, true)</span>
                — fire it earlier or later.
            </dd>
        </dl>
        <p>
            <strong>The course re-solves under every press.</strong> Watch the closest-pass number and keep
            pressing whichever button makes it smaller. The typed fields are still there for fine trims —
            they are the exception now, not the way in.
        </p>
    </section>

    @* ── 5 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
    <section class="ph-step">
        <span class="ph-num" aria-hidden="true">5</span>
        <h3 class="ph-head">The plan ends with an arrival — armed now, flown later</h3>
        <p>
            Open the last step and press
            <span class="ph-chip ph-chip-key">@ArrivalStepRule.ArmButtonLabel("Mars")</span> while the
            burns are still ahead of you. Arming is a decision about the whole trip, not something you have
            to be awake for at the pass, and it arms <em>that body, at that pass</em> — never whatever
            happens to be nearest when the moment comes.
        </p>
        <p class="ph-quote">@ArrivalStepRule.ArmedAndNothingMore</p>
        <p>
            Three burns and one arrival, then zero input. That sentence is what you are plotting towards.
        </p>
    </section>

    @* ── 6 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
    <section class="ph-step">
        <span class="ph-num" aria-hidden="true">6</span>
        <h3 class="ph-head">Reading the badge on the arrival</h3>
        <dl class="ph-pairs">
            <dt class="ph-good">@TheBadge(true) @ArrivalStepRule.StatusValid</dt>
            <dd>Both gates clear — distance and closing speed. The plan ends safely.</dd>

            <dt class="ph-bad">@TheBadge(false) @ArrivalStepRule.StatusInvalid</dt>
            <dd>
                Not a refusal — a to-do list. The row says in numbers how far off you are on each gate.
                Go back to the ± buttons; the numbers say which way.
            </dd>

            <dt class="ph-wait">@ArrivalStepRule.PendingBadge @ArrivalStepRule.StatusNotJudged</dt>
            <dd>
                No verdict, rather than a bad one: the plotted line stops short of the body, so there is
                nothing yet to judge. Stretch <em>Path length</em> — or press <em>auto</em> — until the
                pass is on the line. The panel will never hand you a @TheBadge(false) it has not earned.
            </dd>
        </dl>
    </section>

    <p class="ph-foot">
        The same trip in eight pictures, one drawn sketch each:
        <a href="/help/nav" target="_blank" rel="noopener">📐 Plotting a trip ↗</a> ·
        everything else — piracy, trade, the surface game:
        <a href="/guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">📖 The Captain's Guide ↗</a>
    </p>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The row's own ✓ / ✗, asked of <see cref="ArrivalStepRule.Badge"/> rather than typed — the
    /// glyph is a function of a verdict in the panel, so it is a function of one here too. The numbers in
    /// the check are only there to make a valid or invalid one; nothing on this card reads them.</summary>
    private static string TheBadge(bool valid) => ArrivalStepRule.Badge(
        ArrivalStepRule.Check(
            ArrivalStepRule.ArrivalKind.Orbit,
            "Mars",
            distance: valid ? 0 : 1e12,
            relSpeed: valid ? 0 : 1e5,
            hillRadius: 1));
}
